  :adr/body
  "# Decision

`gftdcojp/network-isekai` gains a MapleStory-shaped 2D side-scrolling MMORPG,
codename 世界樹 / `sekaiju`. Its durable persistence is the Cloudflare-accessed
PostgreSQL-backed Kotobase service, per ADR 260726-gftd-jk-luxury-kotobase-postgres-policy.
The full design lives in the child repository; this entry records the fleet-level
decision and the one reusable pattern that came out of it.

# The reusable pattern: three planes

A realtime multiplayer game on Kotobase splits every fact into exactly one plane.

- **durable** — Kotobase over PostgreSQL. Character, inventory, equipment, skills,
  quests, currency, guild, market, world definition, audit ledger. Append-only,
  content-addressed, CAS-serialized per ref.
- **live** — one Cloudflare Durable Object per world/channel/field leaf. 20 Hz
  authoritative simulation, in memory. Position, velocity, FSM, HP, buffs, mob
  state, chat. No durability; recovery is the last durable checkpoint.
- **client** — prediction and interpolation only. No authority.

A fact enters the durable plane only at a checkpoint boundary, and only through
the Durable Object that currently holds that character's write lease. Movement
ticks, damage packets, mob AI and chat never reach PostgreSQL.

This split is derived from what Kotobase actually is — an append-only commit chain
whose writes are one block insert plus one ref CAS, and whose reads are an indexed
prolly-tree snapshot plus a novelty front — not from taste. Any other realtime
game in this fleet that wants Kotobase durability should start from the same split.

# Two consequences worth carrying to other projects

**One ref, one writer.** Refs are sharded (one per character, per guild, per market
shard, per ledger day) so that exactly one Durable Object writes each ref. A DO is a
single-threaded serializer, so CAS never contends and `commit-serialized!`'s retry
budget is never consumed. The goal is not to handle contention well; it is to make
contention impossible.

**Fencing epochs make duplication structurally impossible.** The write lease lives
inside the character's own ref as `:lease/holder` + `:lease/epoch`. A transaction is
admitted only when it carries the current epoch and holder, so a crashed or
partitioned DO's late writes are fenced. Two Durable Objects cannot hold epoch n at
once, which removes the classic duplicate-login item-duplication class outright.

**Content-addressing turns odds into a proof.** Gacha, equipment scrolls and boss
drops use commit-reveal: the commit hash and the drop-table CID are written to the
append-only ledger before the player acts, the seed and outcome after. Anyone can
re-derive the outcome and confirm the operator did not change the odds afterwards.
This is not available on a mutable SQL table and is treated as a product feature,
not an implementation detail.

# Fold is an operating invariant

Kotobase reads cost indexed-snapshot plus novelty, so an un-folded ref gets slow.
Character refs fold at session close or 64 novelty blocks, ledger refs rotate daily
and fold nightly, market shards fold every five minutes, and an alarm fires at 256
novelty blocks or 24 hours un-folded. Any Kotobase-backed workload in this fleet
needs an equivalent explicit fold owner.

# Phase 0, run 2026-07-26: transport settled, three larger blockers found

**Transport.** Durable Objects reach PostgreSQL through a Worker-side async
`kotobase-storage` provider over Hyperdrive — `kotobase.storage.postgres-worker`
(`.cljs`), living beside the JVM adapter in `kotobase-storage-postgres` so both
share one schema. It passes `kotobase.storage.async-contract/verify` 8/8 against
real PostgreSQL 17.9; the JVM adapter passes its own contract 8/8 on the same
server. The write half of the round trip is proven: 28 field quads committed to
the sekaiju `/world` ref, head matching, blocks and ref row landing in PostgreSQL.

Two candidates were rejected. Routing through `kotobase-server`'s XRPC surface at
kotobase.net would make the durable plane B2-backed, because B2/S3 is what
net-kotobase actually stores into. And the first draft of this entry was **wrong**
about `kotoba-lang/org-postgresql-wire`: that repository points the other way — it
*serves* the PostgreSQL wire protocol on top of kotobase so `psql` can query
kotobase — and cannot reach a PostgreSQL server. Anything citing it as a client
path should be corrected.

Two operational notes worth carrying: the injected query function must tolerate
concurrent statements (kotobase fetches blocks while another statement is in
flight, so a single node-postgres `Client` interleaves results — use a pool, which
is what Hyperdrive hands a Worker anyway), and both Hyperdrive configs on the
Cloudflare account point at RisingWave, a streaming engine, not a transactional
PostgreSQL.

# Blocking prerequisites

1. **The cljs read path cannot read back what the cljs write path wrote.**
   `transact!` and `head` succeed against real PostgreSQL; `q`, `pull`, `datoms`
   and `query` then die on `block-miss` for a block that is demonstrably present
   — confirmed by querying `kotobase_blocks` directly and by calling `-get-blocks`
   on that exact CID, which returns the bytes. The trampoline retries the first
   two misses and the third never reaches the retry handler. Substituting the
   canonical `kotobase.server.trampoline` for `kotobase-engine`'s private
   duplicate fails identically, which rules out the miss-signal encoding and
   points at `kotobase-peer`'s cljs `hydrate-chain`/`hydrate-db` not surfacing
   misses through the promise it returns. This blocks **every Worker-side reader
   in the fleet**, not just this game. Owner: `kotobase-peer`.
2. **The entire `kotobase.storage` layer is unpublished.** `kotobase-storage`,
   `kotobase-storage-s3`, `kotobase-storage-ipfs`, `kotobase-storage-postgres`
   and `kotobase-engine` each have zero commits, no GitHub repository and no
   `manifest/west.yml` entry. `kotobase`'s own `src/kotobase/core.clj` and
   `core.cljs` are untracked, and the `deps.edn` on `kotoba-lang/kotobase` main
   still describes the legacy IStore surface — the one the org policy tells
   products not to depend on. So the Datomic-shaped API that policy names as the
   target does not exist in any fetchable form; a fresh clone or CI run cannot
   build it. Registering `kotobase-storage-postgres` alone is impossible — the
   whole chain has to land first.
3. **`isekai.social` still persists to D1 (ADR-0037 phase 1).** The org policy
   forbids silent D1 fallback for new persistence, so wallet, inventory and save
   need a Kotobase adapter before the game's economy can ship. Until then the game
   fails closed rather than writing to D1.
4. **No transactional PostgreSQL instance is provisioned.** Phase 0 verified
   against a local PostgreSQL 17.9. Choosing and paying for a managed instance is
   an owner decision.

# Status

Design complete. Phase 0 partially done: transport decided and the write path
proven. Implementation waits on prerequisites 1–4, of which 1 and 2 are fleet-wide
concerns that outrank this game."}]
